course: dictionary
lesson: 7685
title: One word, two jobs: protest
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word protest can be a noun, which is a thing, or a verb, which is an action. As a noun, a protest is a strong public show of disagreement, like a march or a rally. As a verb, to protest means to say or show strongly that you disagree. Look at the words around it to see which job it does.
explain: «Across the road there may be a counter-protest with the opposite view, and both sides are allowed to protest peacefully.» Here «protest peacefully» is an action, so protest is a verb. The clue is peacefully: people can show they disagree without fighting. Peaceful protest is a right in many countries.
explain: Protest can also mean to say firmly that something is true when others doubt it. «He protested that he was innocent» means he kept saying he had done nothing wrong. The phrase «under protest» means doing something while complaining, so you can tell how someone feels from the words around it.
fact: A protest is a strong public show of disagreement, such as a march or a rally.
fact: As a verb, protest means to say or show strongly that you disagree or object.
fact: «In protest» means to do something to show that you disagree.
fact: «Under protest» means doing something while complaining about it.
fact: «Peaceful protest» means disagreeing in public without violence, and it is a right in many countries.
exercise: «Across the road there may be a counter-protest with the opposite view.» What does this suggest? | A quiet walk in the park | A school trip | A show of agreement with the crowd | *A protest that answers another protest with the opposite view
exercise: Mia's class heard the trip was cancelled. «We protested!» What does protested mean here? | Ate their lunch | Agreed happily | Went to sleep | *Said strongly that they disagreed
exercise: Which word is a noun for a public show of disagreement, a thing you can join in the street? | jump | quietly | swim | *protest
test: «He protested that he was innocent.» What does protested mean here? | *Said firmly that it was true | Forgot his coat | Ran away from the police | Cried with joy
test: The workers stopped work under protest. What does under protest mean? | Stopped because they were tired and happy | Stopped by mistake and did not notice | *Stopped while complaining | Stopped for lunch
exam: Which sentence uses protest as a verb? | Protest is a word with two parts. | A protest is a strong public show of disagreement. | *The people gathered to protest against the new road. | The protest was loud, with bright signs and a band.
